{:seon.config/cluster
 [:string {:min 1 :seon.db/identity true}]

 :seon.config/applied-manifest-digest
 [:re "^[0-9a-f]{64}$"]

 :seon.config/on-core-error
 [:enum {:seon.config/dial true} :record :panic]

 ; THE ONE LIMIT on an agent evaluation, as a dial. The evaluator takes
 ; it in its request and never defaults it: a deadline literal in code
 ; is what the ruling bans, and a dial with a provenanced default is
 ; what replaces it. Declared here with the other dials so the config
 ; gate and this value schema share one authority.
 :seon.config.eval/time-limit-ms
 [:int {:min 1}]

 ; WHO hears a fault nobody else can be told about. It names an agent
 ; id, and boot seeds `"root"` so the shipped default names something
 ; that really exists. Still OPTIONAL: a manifest may drop it, and
 ; absence is then the state — the error fact is committed and no
 ; escalation message is sent.
 :seon.config.error/escalate-to
 [:string {:min 1 :seon.config/optional true}]

 ; How many faults of one SIGNATURE since this process started before
 ; the same fault also escalates. Counted by query over committed
 ; facts, never a stored tally.
 :seon.config.error/recurrence-limit
 [:int {:min 1}]

 ; THE VIEW. Port 0 means the operating system chooses and the chosen
 ; port is reported — a second cluster just works, and nobody has to
 ; know a number in advance. Pin it when a stable bookmark is wanted.
 :seon.config.web/port
 [:int {:min 0 :max 65535 :seon.config/optional true}]

 ; How often a tab is willing to be repainted. A coalescing FLOOR, not
 ; a timer standing in for an unobservable event: the observable event
 ; is the commit and `listen!` already delivers it. Declared HERE with
 ; the other dials, because a dial has to be inside the config
 ; singleton entity to install as an attribute at all — a declaration
 ; beside its consumer would have been tidier and would not have
 ; installed.
 :seon.config.render/coalesce-ms [:int {:min 0 :max 1000}]

 ; HOW LONG AN AGENT MAY RUN ON ITS OWN STEAM. Unit: consecutive runs
 ; per idle→running episode — runs since (and including) the one
 ; answering the last OUTSIDE trigger, derived purely from committed
 ; facts (the run's opening transaction names its trigger; a trigger
 ; with no :seon.cluster.message/from and no
 ; :seon.cluster.message/about came from outside the population's
 ; autonomous activity — a human, or a schedule fire). No stored
 ; counter exists; an outside trigger's own run IS the reset. ABSENT is
 ; FAIL-CLOSED for agent-sent triggers (the max-chain precedent):
 ; nothing agent-triggered opens, outside triggers open normally.
 :seon.config.run/max-episode-runs
 [:int {:min 1 :seon.config/optional true}]

 ; HOW LONG A CONVERSATION MAY RUN WITHOUT A HUMAN. Unit: agent-to-agent
 ; hops in one causal chain. Delivery IS the wake, so alice messaging
 ; bob wakes bob, whose reply wakes alice — a polite infinite
 ; conversation is a REAL shape, and unlike the error storm it never
 ; repeats a signature, so the recurrence fence cannot see it. Each hop
 ; is also a paid model call, so the bound is a spend bound as much as
 ; a liveness one. Counted by walking committed transaction metadata,
 ; never a stored counter (the quarry stored `hops` on every message
 ; and then needed a second rule to reset it).
 :seon.config.message/max-chain
 [:int {:min 1}]

 ; THE MODEL SEAM, as four dials rather than a map hard-coded in a
 ; drive script. The run loop is installed at BOOT now, so the provider
 ; it calls has to be derivable from facts like everything else it
 ; carries; these are the descriptor row the loop's provider is built
 ; from. The credential is NEVER here — only the NAME of the
 ; environment variable that holds it, which is what keeps a key out of
 ; the database and out of Git.
 :seon.config.ai/endpoint
 [:string {:min 1}]

 :seon.config.ai/model
 [:string {:min 1}]

 ; The positive output budget sent to the provider. It bounds thinking
 ; and visible completion together; the HTTP deadline remains only the
 ; backstop for genuinely unobservable remote state.
 :seon.config.ai/max-tokens
 :seon.ai/max-tokens

 :seon.config.ai/api-key-variable
 [:string {:min 1}]

 ; The deadline on a genuinely unobservable external call — the one
 ; place a clock is the honest mechanism rather than a stand-in for an
 ; event we could have watched.
 :seon.config.ai/timeout-ms
 [:int {:min 1}]

 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; THE BACKUP DESCRIPTOR ROW — the four dials above, a second time, for
 ; the OPTIONAL second target. Three of the four are optional OVERRIDES
 ; that inherit the primary's value, and that is what makes a partial
 ; backup UNREPRESENTABLE rather than merely refused: a backup exists
 ; exactly when `:seon.config.ai.backup/model` is present, so there is
 ; no state in which three backup dials are configured and the fourth
 ; silently voids them. It also makes the common case honest — a backup
 ; model at the same provider is ONE dial, not four copied lines.
 ;
 ; This is a deliberate departure from research/model-failover
 ; §"Provider descriptors" option 2 (provider + model-target ENTITY rows
 ; joined by refs). The manifest is a flat dial map validated key by
 ; key, and `:seon.db/ref` admits an eid, a tempid or a lookup ref —
 ; never an inline map — so entity rows would need the config gate, the
 ; reconciler's desired-row identity rule and the ref candidate form all
 ; changed at once. Two roles do not need a join table; when a third
 ; role or per-agent overrides arrive, THAT is the change that earns the
 ; entity rows.
 :seon.config.ai.backup/model
 [:string {:min 1 :seon.config/optional true}]

 :seon.config.ai.backup/endpoint
 [:string {:min 1 :seon.config/optional true}]

 :seon.config.ai.backup/api-key-variable
 [:string {:min 1 :seon.config/optional true}]

 :seon.config.ai.backup/timeout-ms
 [:int {:min 1 :seon.config/optional true}]

 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; THE BACKOFF STRATEGY. It runs on exactly ONE path — an unpaid,
 ; transient failure with NO backup configured — and it is data so that
 ; the schedule is a value a seeded property pins rather than a constant
 ; in a call site.
 :seon.config.ai.retry/base-delay-ms
 :seon.ai.retry/base-delay-ms

 :seon.config.ai.retry/multiplier
 :seon.ai.retry/multiplier

 :seon.config.ai.retry/jitter-fraction
 :seon.ai.retry/jitter-fraction

 :seon.config.ai.retry/maximum-delay-ms
 :seon.ai.retry/maximum-delay-ms

 :seon.config.ai.retry/maximum-retries
 :seon.ai.retry/maximum-retries

 :seon.config.ai.retry/maximum-total-delay-ms
 :seon.ai.retry/maximum-total-delay-ms

 :seon.config/apply-request
 [:map {:closed true}
  ; A live Datahike connection is a genuine third-party boundary.
  [:seon.config/connection :any]
  [:seon.config/manifest :seon.config/manifest]
  [:seon.boot/cluster-name :seon.boot/cluster-name]]}
